<h3>Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
<p>Does Ozempic directly cause loose skin?</p>
<p>Loose skin is more accurately associated with substantial or rapid weight loss rather than being a unique direct effect of one medication. Skin may not contract fully after body volume decreases.</p>
<p>Is body contouring the same as liposuction?</p>
<p>No. Liposuction removes localized fat. Body-contouring surgery after major weight loss often involves removing significant amounts of excess skin. Some procedures may incorporate liposuction, but it cannot substitute for skin removal when substantial laxity is present.</p>
<p>What is the most common surgery after GLP-1 weight loss?</p>
<p>There is no single procedure appropriate for everyone. However, ASPS reported that tummy tuck was the body procedure most frequently discussed in GLP-1-related consultations among surveyed member surgeons, followed closely by breast surgery, arm lift and thigh lift.</p>
<p>Can several procedures be done at once?</p>
<p>Sometimes, but more is not necessarily better. The safe number and combination of procedures depend on medical history, surgical time, extent of tissue removal and other factors. Extensive post-weight-loss body contouring is often staged.</p>
<p>Should I stop Ozempic, Wegovy, Mounjaro or Zepbound before surgery?</p>
<p>Do not make this decision yourself. Medication management should be coordinated between your prescribing clinician, surgeon and anesthesiologist.</p>
<p>How much does body contouring cost in Mexico?</p>
<p>Costs vary significantly by procedure. Current PlacidWay clinic data shows tummy-tuck quotes in Mexico from approximately $3,950 to $12,900, while larger multi-procedure plans can cost considerably more.</p>
<p>Is Mexico safe for plastic surgery?</p>
<p>Mexico has qualified plastic surgeons and established private hospitals, but quality varies by provider and facility. Safety should be evaluated at the surgeon and facility level rather than assuming that every clinic within a country meets the same standards.</p>
<p>Is Mexico better than Turkey for body contouring?</p>
<p>For many US and Canadian patients, Mexico may be more practical because of travel distance and follow-up access. For UK or European patients, Turkey may offer the geographic advantage. Surgeon expertise and aftercare should ultimately be more important than destination popularity.</p>
<p>How soon after reaching my goal weight should I consider surgery?</p>
<p>Weight should generally be stable before body contouring, but there is no single timeline appropriate to every patient. A plastic surgeon should evaluate your weight trajectory, medical health, medication use and nutritional status.</p>
